    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The area was a place of farming and fishing villages under Chinese dynasties until the Qing ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1842 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, and Britain added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> in 1860 and the New Territories on a 99-year lease in 1898.</li><li>Refugees from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, especially after 1949, supplied the labour and capital that turned a trading port into an industrial territory in the 1950s and later into a financial and shipping centre.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-british-joint-declaration">Sino-British Joint Declaration</a> of 1984 the United Kingdom handed Hong Kong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at midnight on 1 July 1997, after 156 years of British rule.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name first appears in English in 1780 as &quot;He-Ong-Kong&quot; and originally referred to a small inlet near Aberdeen; it is generally believed to render the Cantonese hēung góng, &quot;fragrant harbour&quot; or &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> harbour&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

