    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1279</span><span class="rf-text">The southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court, briefly based near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> City, is defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a> by the Yuan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1842</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> cedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> to the United Kingdom in perpetuity</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text">The Convention of Peking gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> Peninsula and Stonecutters Island</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> obtains a 99-year lease of the New Territories</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> attacks Hong Kong on 8 December and the British surrender on 25 December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1984</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-british-joint-declaration">Sino-British Joint Declaration</a> provides for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handover-of-hong-kong">handover of Hong Kong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1997</span><span class="rf-text">Hong Kong is handed over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at midnight on 1 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2020</span><span class="rf-text">The Hong Kong national security law comes into effect on 1 July</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hong Kong stands on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s southern coast, on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> estuary, and consists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> Peninsula, the New Territories, Lantau Island and over 200 other islands. The earliest known human traces there date to 35,000 to 39,000 years ago, from knapped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> found at Wong Tei Tung on the Sai Kung Peninsula in deposits dated by optical luminescence. By the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period the region had been widely occupied. Early inhabitants are believed to have been Austronesians and later the Yue people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation had been introduced by the Late Neolithic period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> settlers made coarse and hard pottery, quartz and stone jewellery and small bronze implements.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> brought the area into China after conquering the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>. It was then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> kingdom and was recaptured by China after the Han conquest. In the 13th century the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court was briefly based in modern Kowloon City, where the Sung Wong Toi site stands, before its defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a> in 1279. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> seven large families had settled in the region and owned most of the land. The name, first romanised as "He-Ong-Kong" in 1780, originally referred to a small inlet between Aberdeen Island and the south coast of Hong Kong Island, where British sailors first met local fishermen.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Hong Kong&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, Prehistory and Imperial China:</p><ul><li>John Carroll, A Concise History of Hong Kong (Rowman &amp; Littlefield, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hong Kong was originally an area of farming and fishing villages. After the British took the island, administrative buildings went up quickly in 1842, but piracy, disease and hostile Qing policies at first kept merchants away. Conditions improved in the 1850s, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> sent many Chinese refugees, including wealthy merchants, to the colony. By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a> Hong Kong had grown from a transient outpost into a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a>. The Hongkong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> Bank opened in 1865, the first gas company in 1862 and the first electric company in 1890. The population rose from 530,000 in 1916 to 725,000 in 1925, and the University of Hong Kong was founded in 1911.</p><p>After 1949 skilled migrants and capital, especially from Shanghai, and a large pool of cheap labour revived the economy. In the 1950s Hong Kong changed from entrepôt trade to manufacturing, making goods such as buttons, artificial flowers, umbrellas, textiles and plastics. Public housing estates, the Independent Commission Against Corruption, founded in 1974, and the Mass Transit Railway were set up in the post-war decades. As costs rose, manufacturers moved to the mainland, and by the early 1990s Hong Kong had become a financial centre and shipping hub.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Hong Kong&#8221; cites where it covers Prehistory and Imperial China, Demographics, Economy:</p><ul><li>John Carroll, A Concise History of Hong Kong (Rowman &amp; Littlefield, 2007)</li><li>John Nguyet Erni, Understanding South Asian Minorities in Hong Kong (Hong Kong University Press, 2014)</li><li>Rikkie Yeung, Moving Millions: The Commercial Success and Political Controversies of Hong Kong's Railways (Hong Kong University Press, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping.jpg" alt="Deng Xiaoping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deng_Xiaoping_at_the_arrival_ceremony_for_the_Vice_Premier_of_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> the area was governed from Panyu County, then it was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> and was recaptured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the Han conquest. In later centuries it fell under Panyu, Bao'an, Dongguan and finally Xin'an County, until the British arrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> on 25 and 26 January 1841 under the Convention of Chuenpi, signed by Charles Elliot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-qi">Qishan</a>, which neither government ratified. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> of 29 August 1842 ceded the island in perpetuity and it became a crown colony. The Convention of Peking of 1860 added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> Peninsula and Stonecutters Island, and in 1898 Britain obtained a 99-year lease of the New Territories.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied Hong Kong from 23 December 1941 to 15 August 1945, with Isogai Rensuke as its first Japanese governor. The British resumed control in 1945, and the colony kept its form of government through the post-war decades. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murray-maclehose">Murray MacLehose</a> raised the question of Hong Kong's future with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> in 1979. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chris-patten">Chris Patten</a>, appointed in 1992, was the last British governor. The Legislative Council became fully elected for the first time in 1995. At midnight on 1 July 1997, after 156 years of British rule, the United Kingdom handed Hong Kong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Tung Chee Hwa became its first chief executive.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Hong Kong&#8221; cites where it covers Government and politics:</p><ul><li>Simon N.M. Young, Electing Hong Kong's Chief Executive (Hong Kong University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="Sun Yat-sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AD%99%E4%B8%AD%E5%B1%B1%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">上海波尔照相馆, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The indigenous inhabitants before 1898 included the Punti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanka">Tanka</a> and Hoklo, who had lived in the area for many years. When the British arrived, the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> was about 7,450, mostly Tanka fishermen and Hakka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> burners in coastal villages. Some clans built walled villages for protection from bandits, rival clans and wild animals. Salt, pearls and fishing had long provided a living. Cantonese later became the predominant language, spoken by 93.7 per cent of the population today, with English the other official language.</p><p>Christian missionaries founded many schools and churches. The Hong Kong College of Medicine for Chinese, founded in 1887, graduated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> in 1892. The third bubonic plague pandemic reached Hong Kong in 1894 and gave a pretext for racial zoning through the Peak Reservation Ordinance. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> squatter camps spread across the territory for the many migrants. A fire at Shek Kip Mei led Governor Alexander Grantham to a plan for multi-storey public housing, the start of the high-rise building for which the city is known. The Hong Kong Flu of 1968 infected 15 per cent of the population. Cinema and Cantopop grew from the late 1940s and 1970s respectively, and the territory's culture is a hybrid of Eastern and Western traditions.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Hong Kong&#8221; cites where it covers Architecture, Culture, Cuisine, Cinema:</p><ul><li>John Carroll, A Concise History of Hong Kong (Rowman &amp; Littlefield, 2007)</li><li>Yiu-wai Chu, Hong Kong Cantopop: A Concise History (Hong Kong University Press, 2017)</li><li>Charlie Q.L. Xue, Hong Kong Architecture 1945–2015: From Colonial to Global (Springer, 2016)</li><li>Lucy M. Long, Ethnic American Food Today: A Cultural Encyclopedia (Rowman &amp; Littlefield, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Third Pandemic of bubonic plague struck in 1894. In April 1899 the residents of Kam Tin rebelled against the colonial government and defended the walled village of Kat Hing Wai until British troops blasted open its iron gate. The Japanese attack began on 8 December 1941, eight hours after the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>. British, Canadian and Indian troops and the Hong Kong Volunteer Defence Forces resisted but were outnumbered, and on 25 December, called Black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> by locals, Governor Mark Aitchison Young surrendered. During the occupation food was rationed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> ran high, and an estimated 10,000 civilians were executed, while many others were tortured, raped or mutilated. The population fell from 1.6 million before the war to 600,000 in 1945.</p><p>In the 1967 riots pro-PRC protesters clashed with the colonial government; as many as 51 people were killed and 802 injured, and police and the British military defused as many as 8,000 home-made bombs. After the handover the government used large foreign exchange reserves to hold the currency peg in the Asian financial crisis of 1997, and the territory then had an H5N1 avian-flu outbreak and the 2003 SARS epidemic. A march against the Article 23 security bill drew as many as 750,000 people in 2003 and the bill was shelved. Protests over the electoral system in 2014 became known as the Umbrella Revolution. Protests against an extradition bill began in June 2019, and organisers claimed more than three million took part.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher.jpg" alt="Margaret Thatcher" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_Thatcher_(1983).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rob Bogaerts, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hong Kong was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-asian-tigers">Four Asian Tigers</a> and the first of them to industrialise, in the 1950s. Between 1961 and 1997 its gross domestic product increased by a factor of 180. Its legal system is based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a>, continuing the tradition of British rule, and vehicles still drive on the left. Many buildings of the colonial and earlier periods survive, among them the Tin Hau Temple, first built in 1012 and rebuilt in 1266, the oldest existing structure in the territory, and Flagstaff House of 1846, the oldest Western-style building.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-british-joint-declaration">Sino-British Joint Declaration</a> of 19 December 1984, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> and Zhao Ziyang, promised that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> would keep Hong Kong's economic and political systems for 50 years after the handover under the principle of one country, two systems. The Basic Law, accepted on 4 April 1990, serves as the region's constitution. The national security law came into effect on 1 July 2020, and in March 2021 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a> changed the electoral system. Hong Kong is today a special administrative region of China of about 7.5 million residents, with its own currency, immigration policy and public finances.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua<span>1765</span></a><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne<span>1788</span></a><a href="/songs/the-minstrel-boy">The Minstrel Boy<span>1813</span></a><a href="/songs/blow-the-man-down">Blow the Man Down<span>1867</span></a><a href="/songs/now-is-the-hour">Now Is the Hour<span>1913</span></a><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers<span>1935</span></a><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland<span>1950</span></a><a href="/songs/the-moon-represents-my-heart">The Moon Represents My Heart<span>1973</span></a><a href="/songs/el-pueblo-unido-jamas-sera-vencido">El pueblo unido jamás será vencido<span>1973</span></a><a href="/songs/tian-mi-mi">Tian Mi Mi<span>1979</span></a><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong<span>2019</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hong Kong, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s southern coast, was an area of farming and fishing villages until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took the island in 1841 and added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> and the New Territories. Refugees and trade made it an industrial and financial centre. It returned to China in 1997.</em></p>

